WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.002
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:08.677
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.977 --> 00:00:18.328
Unit two: Power and Legitimacy. In this unit, you will analyse forms of power and what makes authority legitimate.

4
00:00:19.317 --> 00:00:20.364
hegemony.

5
00:00:21.064 --> 00:00:26.338
hegemony means dominance of one group or state over others, often through consent.

6
00:00:26.638 --> 00:00:29.938
Cultural hegemony shapes what people see as normal.

7
00:00:30.638 --> 00:00:33.093
Your turn. Say: hegemony.

8
00:00:35.793 --> 00:00:36.759
hegemony.

9
00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:39.169
authoritarianism.

10
00:00:39.869 --> 00:00:45.630
authoritarianism means a system demanding obedience and limiting personal freedom.

11
00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:50.263
Political scientists study why authoritarianism persists.

12
00:00:50.963 --> 00:00:54.289
Your turn. Say: authoritarianism.

13
00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:58.304
authoritarianism.

14
00:00:59.317 --> 00:01:00.341
technocracy.

15
00:01:01.041 --> 00:01:04.132
technocracy means government by technical experts.

16
00:01:04.432 --> 00:01:09.009
Supporters of technocracy value expertise over popularity.

17
00:01:09.709 --> 00:01:12.129
Your turn. Say: technocracy.

18
00:01:14.829 --> 00:01:16.051
technocracy.

19
00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.283
oligarchy.

20
00:01:18.983 --> 00:01:22.225
oligarchy means rule by a small group of powerful people.

21
00:01:22.525 --> 00:01:25.860
Aristotle warned that oligarchy serves only the few.

22
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.549
Your turn. Say: oligarchy.

23
00:01:32.249 --> 00:01:33.273
oligarchy.

24
00:01:34.283 --> 00:01:35.447
plutocracy.

25
00:01:36.147 --> 00:01:38.657
plutocracy means rule by the wealthy.

26
00:01:38.957 --> 00:01:42.571
Critics fear that money in politics leads to plutocracy.

27
00:01:43.271 --> 00:01:46.364
Your turn. Say: plutocracy.

28
00:01:49.064 --> 00:01:50.193
plutocracy.

29
00:01:51.183 --> 00:01:52.986
meritocratic ideal.

30
00:01:53.686 --> 00:01:58.483
meritocratic ideal means the belief that positions should go to the most capable.

31
00:01:58.783 --> 00:02:02.791
The meritocratic ideal assumes equal opportunity.

32
00:02:03.491 --> 00:02:07.328
Your turn. Say: meritocratic ideal.

33
00:02:10.028 --> 00:02:11.563
meritocratic ideal.

34
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:13.574
mandate.

35
00:02:14.274 --> 00:02:17.946
mandate means the authority given by voters or a higher body.

36
00:02:18.246 --> 00:02:21.522
A government needs a clear mandate for major reforms.

37
00:02:22.222 --> 00:02:24.596
Your turn. Say: mandate.

38
00:02:27.296 --> 00:02:28.309
mandate.

39
00:02:29.317 --> 00:02:30.666
patronage network.

40
00:02:31.366 --> 00:02:36.442
patronage network means a system of favours exchanged for loyalty.

41
00:02:36.742 --> 00:02:40.089
Patronage networks can undermine fair competition.

42
00:02:40.789 --> 00:02:44.161
Your turn. Say: patronage network.

43
00:02:46.861 --> 00:02:48.315
patronage network.

44
00:02:49.317 --> 00:02:51.676
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:51.976 --> 00:02:56.274
Ms. Sarah, what makes power legitimate rather than simply strong?

46
00:02:57.583 --> 00:03:03.394
Broadly speaking, consent. Power tends to be seen as legitimate when people accept it as fair.

47
00:03:04.683 --> 00:03:06.753
Even if it isn't democratic?

48
00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:14.023
Arguably. Some technocracies claim legitimacy through results rather than elections.

49
00:03:15.317 --> 00:03:18.408
But surely that risks sliding into oligarchy?

50
00:03:19.717 --> 00:03:25.025
It may well do, particularly where patronage networks replace fair competition.

51
00:03:26.317 --> 00:03:28.258
And the meritocratic ideal?

52
00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:35.868
It is, to my mind, attractive in theory, though it presupposes genuinely equal opportunity.

53
00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:41.772
Grammar focus: Hedging and stance in academic writing.

54
00:03:42.072 --> 00:03:47.044
At C2, stance markers show exactly how strongly you commit to a claim.

55
00:03:47.344 --> 00:03:53.363
Broadly speaking, legitimacy depends on consent. It may well slide into oligarchy.

56
00:03:53.663 --> 00:03:56.626
The ideal presupposes equal opportunity.

57
00:03:56.926 --> 00:04:03.349
C2 writers signal their stance precisely: how certain they are and how far they commit to a claim.

58
00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:07.327
Now read and listen. Why Do People Obey?.

59
00:04:07.627 --> 00:04:13.481
The sociologist Max Weber distinguished three sources of legitimate authority.

60
00:04:14.783 --> 00:04:22.994
Traditional authority rests on custom, charismatic authority on personal appeal, and legal authority on rules.

61
00:04:24.283 --> 00:04:30.184
Broadly speaking, modern states claim legitimacy through law and a democratic mandate.

62
00:04:31.483 --> 00:04:37.059
Yet legitimacy can erode when patronage networks override fair procedures.

63
00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:45.597
Some theorists argue that hegemony operates most effectively when power feels natural rather than imposed.

64
00:04:46.883 --> 00:04:52.679
Technocracy offers an alternative claim to legitimacy, based on expertise and performance.

65
00:04:53.983 --> 00:04:59.675
Critics contend, however, that expertise without accountability may drift towards oligarchy.

66
00:05:00.983 --> 00:05:06.280
Legitimacy, it would appear, must be continually earned rather than simply possessed.

67
00:05:07.583 --> 00:05:10.410
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.779
Rule by the wealthy is blank.

69
00:05:13.079 --> 00:05:17.034
A, plutocracy. B, technocracy. C, mandate.

70
00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:23.514
The answer is A: plutocracy.

71
00:05:25.017 --> 00:05:27.248
Which phrase marks the writer's stance?

72
00:05:27.548 --> 00:05:31.689
A, To my mind. B, Next. C, Because.

73
00:05:35.683 --> 00:05:38.275
The answer is A: To my mind.

74
00:05:39.783 --> 00:05:42.491
The plan blank well fail without funding.

75
00:05:42.791 --> 00:05:46.224
A, may. B, must. C, should.

76
00:05:50.217 --> 00:05:52.135
The answer is A: may.

77
00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:57.345
A system of favours exchanged for loyalty is a blank.

78
00:05:57.645 --> 00:06:03.190
A, patronage network. B, meritocratic ideal. C, hegemony.

79
00:06:07.183 --> 00:06:10.216
The answer is A: patronage network.

80
00:06:11.717 --> 00:06:16.503
According to the text, how many sources of authority did Weber identify?

81
00:06:16.803 --> 00:06:19.992
A, two. B, three. C, four.

82
00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:26.424
The answer is B: three.

83
00:06:27.917 --> 00:06:34.319
Now it's your turn. Part one: What makes a government legitimate in the eyes of its citizens?

84
00:06:39.619 --> 00:06:45.485
Part two: Use stance markers and hedges; present two criteria and evaluate one.

85
00:06:51.785 --> 00:07:04.851
Broadly speaking, I would point to fair procedures and tangible results. Of the two, fairness arguably matters more in the long term, since results without accountability may well erode public trust.

86
00:07:05.151 --> 00:07:08.207
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:09.217 --> 00:07:14.528
Great work today! You learned eight new words and hedging and stance in academic writing.

88
00:07:14.828 --> 00:07:19.314
Today's value is responsibility. Help at home and do your jobs.

89
00:07:19.614 --> 00:07:21.976
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

90
00:07:22.276 --> 00:07:25.242
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

